## 1. Introduction
Research on the exploration of metal organic frameworks (MOFs) in electrochemical immunoassay, which is also known as a functional tool to establish diversiform sensing interfaces, has been growing tremendously over the past years [1–3]. With an ordered combination between metal centers and organic ligands, MOFs materials have many unique performance advantages, such as controllable pore size, modified pore surface, low density and high surface area, which make the new porous material have widely used in many fields like adsorption [4], photocatalysis [5] and catalysis [6–7]. It is these versatile framework functionalities that endow MOFs with a flexible strategy to participate in the assembly of immunosensor [8–9], especially for the preparation of MOFs-based immunoprobe and substrate [10–12]. Regrettably, most previous developments of MOFs are adopted to be directly modified on the electrode surface and subsequently resonated with some considerable challenges [13–15]. Firstly, since the coordination between metal ions and organic ligands cannot support the free passage of electrons, a myriad of MOFs are tormented by poor conductivity for the surface modification of electrodes [16– 18]. Such restriction has focused on searching for the assistance of other nonnative conductive materials so that its preparation becomes fussy and time-cunsuming [19–20]. Furthermore, due to the deficiency of available groups, organic dyes like methylene blue (MB) and thionine as common electroactive species are always loaded in MOFs through van der Waals adsorption to indicate the content of an analytical sample [21–22]. In comparison of firm chemical coupling, such vulnerable loading easily causes the leakage of signal molecules to decrease the reproducibility of electrochemical signal [23–24]. For the sake of addressing above issues, ongoing intensive efforts should be made for the improvement of MOFs in electrochemical immunosensing.
Recently, reformative measures for MOFs have been extensively researched and many of works are focusing primarily on the improvement of organic ligand and post-modification of functional materials [25–26]. Particularly for the latter, lots of MOFs are integrated with some versatile molecules to be possessed a series of stimulated responses under the conditions like pH, light, magnetism, and metal ion, which allow them to have the potential to be applied in pharmacy, sensing, etc [27–29]. With this inspiration, a blessing in disguise will be obtained if we tactfully introduce some efficacious biomolecules into the modification of MOFs surface and design MOFs as a signal molecules loader in solution which is unshackled from electrode surface modification [30–31].
UIO- $66\mathrm{-NH}_{2}$ , as one of the excellent matrix materials, is widely applied in constructing various electrochemical immunosensing interfaces by virtue of its high porosity and abundant surficial amino groups. With an ordered combination of zirconium ions and 2-amino terephthalic acid $(\mathsf{N H}_{2}-\mathsf{H}_{2}\mathsf{B D C})$ , it is usually used to load electrochemical active species and can be functionalized by biomolecules. In this work, a proton-activated annunciator based on i-motif DNA functionalized ${\mathsf{U I O-}}66{\mathsf{-N H}}_{2}$ was proposed for designing electrochemical immunosensor (squamous cell carcinoma antigen (SCCA) was used as model analyte). For the source of stimulation, in the beginning, ZIF-8 encapsulated glucose oxidase (ZIF-8/GOx) was developed as an immunoprobe to establish a quantitative relationship with the content of analysis target in test tube and produced increasing protons when they were incubated with glucose to generated gluconic acid [32–33]. After the transference of protons to a solution containing i-motif DNA functionalized UIO-66- $\left.\mathrm{NH}_{2}\right.$ [29–30] these improved MOFs as a kind of stimuli-responsive carriers that can be de-archived by protons to release preloaded MB and dissociative MB molecules were further fixed on the gold-reduced graphene oxide (Au-rGO)-improved electrode. Such applications of reformative MOFs not merely eliminate the restrictions of its poor conductivity on electrode surface but also avoid the conventional insecure loading of electroactive species. In view of this, the method was expected to offer serviceable information for the exploitation of MOFs-based materials and further provided a universal and facile strategy for future designing of electrochemical sensor.
## 2. Experimental section
## 2.1. Materials and chemicals
SCCA, coated anti-SCCA $(\mathsf{A b}_{1})$ , labeled anti-SCCA $(\mathsf{A b}_{2})$ , Cyfra21- 1 (CK-19), Neuron specific enolase (NSE), human immunoglobulin G (IgG) and prostate specific antigen (PSA) were obtained from Linc-Bio Science Co. Ltd. (Shanghai, China) and used with no further purification. 1-Ethyl-3-( $3^{\prime}$ -dimethylaminopropyl) carbodiimide (EDC, $\geq99\%$ , AR), bovine serum albumin (BSA, standard grade) and $N\mathrm{.}$ -Hydroxysuccinimide (NHS, $98\%$ , AR) were purchased from Beijing Xinjingke Bioechnology Co. Ltd. (Beijing, China). Ascorbic acid (AA, $99\%$ , AR), dopamine hydrochloride (DA, $99\%$ ， AR), 2-methylimidazole (2-MI, $99\%$ ), Zinc nitrate hexahydrate (Zn $(\mathsf{N O}_{3})_{2}{\cdot}6\mathsf{H}_{2}0$ , $98\%$ ), zirconium chloride $\left(Z\mathrm{rCl}_{4}\right.$ , $98\%$ , ${\mathsf{N H}}_{2}–{\mathsf{H}}_{2}{\mathsf{B D C}}$ $(99\%)$ , GOx ( $99\%$ , AR), sodium citrate $(99.8\%$ , AR), hydrogen tetrachloroaurate (III) hydrate $({\mathrm{HAuCl}}_{4}{\cdot}{\bf x}{\mathrm{H}}_{2}0$ , $99\%$ , AR) and sodium chloraurate $\mathrm{\TaAuCl_{4}}$ , $99\%$ , AR) were achieved from Alfa Aesar (Tianjin, China). Graphene Oxide (GO, $99\%$ , AR) was obtained from Nanjing JCNANO Tech Co. Ltd (Nanjing, China). MB (pure), N, Ndimethylformamide (DMF, $\geq99.5\%$ , AR), potassium chloride (KCl, $\geq99.5\%$ , AR) and sodium borohydride $(N a B H_{4}$ , $98\%$ was got from Tianjin Guangfu Fine Chemical Research Institute (Tianjin, China). All oligonucleotides were synthesized and purified via HPLC by Sangon Biotechnology Co. Ltd. (Shanghai, China) (The sequence of oligonucleotides is displayed as follows.
DNA1: $5^{\prime}$ –COOH- ${\cdot}(\mathsf{C H}_{2})_{6}$ -AGTAGGGTTAGG- ${\cdot}3^{\prime}$ DNA2: $5^{\prime}$ -CCCTAACCCTAACCCTAACCCTACT- $3^{\prime}$
## 2.2. Apparatus
Scanning electron microscope (SEM) and energy disperse spectroscopy (EDS) were all implented on Hitachi S-4800 SEM. Transmission electron microscope (TEM) images were taken from JEOL-100CX. X-ray powder diffraction (XRD) was executed on a $\mathrm{D}/\mathrm{Max}~2500~\mathrm{V}/\mathrm{PC}~\$ X-ray diffractometer with Cu $(40\mathrm{\kV},200\mathrm{\mA})$ radiation. X-ray photoelectron spectroscopy (XPS) was conducted on an Escalab 250x-ray Photoelectron Spectroscope (Thermofisher, American). UV–vis measurements were carried out by a UV-2550 UV–Vis spectrophotometer (Shimadzu. Janpan). CHI-660E electrochemical workstation (Chenhua, Shanghai) and Infrared spectroscopy (IR) were also used for this study and three-electrode system was applied to measurement.
## 2.3. Preparation of UIO-66- ${\cdot}N H_{2}$
$0.0467\texttt{g}\texttt{Z r C l}_{4}$ and $0.0363~\mathrm{~g~NH_{2}{-}B D}$ C were dissolved in $20.42~\mathrm{mL}$ DMF and the obtained homogenous solution was sonicated for $10\mathrm{min}$ . Then, $4.58\mathrm{mL}$ acetic acid was added into the mixture, stirring it for $10~\mathrm{{min}}$ . Finally, the mixture was transferred in $50~\mathrm{mL}$ hydrothermal reactor at $120^{\circ}{\mathsf{C}}$ for $^{12\mathrm{~h~}}$ The obtained product was washed twice with DMF and ethanol respectively. Then the production was dried at $80~^{\circ}C$ for $^{12\mathrm{~h~}}$ .
## 2.4. Synthesis of MB@UIO-66- ${\cdot}N H_{2}$
Prior to fabrication of $\mathsf{M B@U I O-66-N H_{2}}$ , EDC and NHS were diluted to $10~\mathrm{mg~mL^{-1}}$ using deionized water. For preparation of $\mathsf{M B@U I O-66-N H_{2}}$ , $500~\upmu\mathrm{L}$ EDC, $500~\upmu\mathrm{L}$ NHS, 2 mL $100~\upmu\mathrm{M}$ DNA1 stock solution were mixed and stirred for $30~\mathrm{{min}}$ to activate the carboxyl on DNA. After that, $35~\mathrm{mg}$ prepared ${\mathsf{U I O}}{-}66{-}{\mathsf{N H}}_{2}$ was added in the mixture and sufficiently stirred at $37^{\circ}\mathsf C$ for $^{15\mathrm{~h~}}$ to form single-stranded DNA modified ${\mathsf{U I O-}}66{\mathsf{-N H}}_{2}$ (ssDNA@UIO$66\substack{-\Delta\mathsf{N}}\mathrm{H}_{2}.$ ). The obtained $\mathsf{s s D N A@U I O-66-N H_{2}}$ was washed with deionized water three times through centrifugation and redispersed in $1.0\ \mathrm{\mL}$ water for further application. After that, $200~\upmu\mathrm{L}~1~\mathrm{mg}~\mathrm{mL}^{-1}~.$ MB solution was dropped into above solution and subsequently stirred for $^{12\mathrm{~h~}}$ . Finally, DNA2 stocking solution was added to incubate for $^{3\mathrm{h}}$ . After these steps, the obtained products need to wash with deionized water and re-dispersed in $1.0\mathrm{mL}$ water.
## 2.5. Preparation of ZIF-8 and ZIF-8/Au NPs/anti-SCCA (ZIF-8/Au NPs/ $A b_{2},$
ZIF-8 was synthesized according to the literature with some adaptations [34]. $200~{\upmu\mathrm{g}}$ GOx and $0.1149\mathrm{~g~}2\mathrm{-MI}$ were dissolved in $1~\mathrm{mL}$ deionized water. After stirring about $10\mathrm{min}$ to give a clear solution, $1\ \mathrm{mL}20\ \mathrm{mM}$ zinc acetate solution was quickly injected into the clear mixture, stirring for overnight at room temperature. After that, products were washed with deionized water three times and dried.
For the synthesis of ZIF-8/gold nanoparticles/labeling antibody, $1\ \mathrm{mg}\ \mathrm{ZIF}{-8}$ was dissolved in $1~\mathrm{mL}$ deionized water. $40~{\upmu\mathrm{L}}~0.025\%$ $\mathsf{N a B H}_{4}$ and $50~\upmu\mathrm{L}$ $\mathsf{N a A u C l}_{4}$ solution was added and stirred for several hours at room temperature. The obtained product was collected by centrifugation and washed to disperse in $1~\mathrm{\mL}$ deionized water. Then, $50\upmu\mathrm{L}200\upmu\mathrm{g}\mathrm{mL}^{-1}\up A\mathrm{b}_{2}$ was added in above solution and stirred for overnight at room temperature. Finally, the collected nanoparticles were treated with 1 mL $1\%$ BSA for $^{1\mathrm{~h~}}$ to block any possible remaining active sites.
2.6. Preparation of anti-SCCA functionalized $F e_{3}O_{4}$ nanoparticles $(A b_{1^{-}}$ $F e_{3}O_{4}N P s)$
Firstly, $3.0\ \mathrm{mg}$ carboxylated $\mathsf{F e}_{3}0_{4}$ was dispersed into $1\ \mathrm{mL}$ deionized water containing $0.4\mathrm{~M~}$ EDC and $0.1\mathrm{~M~}$ NHS, stirring for $30~\mathrm{{min}}$ at $37^{\circ}\mathsf{C}$ [35]. Then, the activated product was washed three times and 1 m $\cdot200~{\upmu\mathrm{g}}~\mathrm{mL}^{-1}~\mathsf{A b}$ was added and stirred for sequentially stirred for $^\mathrm{~1~h~}$ to form $\mathsf{A b}_{1}\mathsf{-F e}_{3}\mathsf{O}_{4}$ . Finally, $1\mathrm{\mL}1\%$ BSA was added to block any possible remaining active sites. After centrifugation and washing, the obtained product was redispersed into $1~\mathrm{mL}$ deionized water for further application.
## 2.7. Synthesis of Au-rGO and fabrication of Au-rGO based electrochemical sensing interface
Preparation of Au-rGO can refer to the previous work of our group [36]. Briefly, 4 m $\mathrm{\Omega}_{\mathrm{L}}1\mathrm{\mg}\mathrm{mL}^{-1}(\mathrm{\Omega}$ GO suspension was mixed with $25\mathrm{\mL0.05\wt{\%}H A u C l_{4}}$ for $^{2\mathrm{~h~}}$ . Then, $1.2\mathrm{\mL}0.2\mathrm{\m}$ sodium citrate solution was added to the mixture, and refluxed about $^\textrm{\scriptsize1h}$ . The product was centrifuged, simultaneously washing with deionized water and finally diluting to $8~\mathrm{mL}$ ultrapure water. For the fabrication of improved electrode modified Au-rGO, $20~\upmu\mathrm{L}$ prepared AurGO solution was drop-casted on the electrode surface to form a film with drying.
## 2.8. Reaction in test tube
Immunoreaction was processed in a tube. $30~\upmu\mathrm{L}$ $\mathrm{\mathfrak{L}A b}_{1}\mathrm{-Fe}_{3}0_{4}$ NPs was added in a $200~\ensuremath{\upmu\mathrm{L}}$ tube and $30~\upmu\mathrm{L}$ SCCA with various concentrations were simultaneously added to incubate for $40\ \mathrm{min}$ at $37^{\circ}\mathsf C$ , followed by rinsing three times with deionized water. Later, $30~\ensuremath{\upmu\mathrm{L}}$ ZIF-8/Au ${\mathsf{N P s}}/{\mathsf{A b}}_{2}$ was added and incubated for another
$40~\mathrm{min}$ at $37^{\circ}\mathsf C$ Eventually, a sandwich-type conjugate was collected by magnetic separation and rinsing.
## 2.9. Process of electrochemical measurement
Firstly, $30~\upmu\mathrm{L}~50~\mathrm{\mM}$ glucose solution was mixed with the sandwich-type conjugate previously collected and incubated for $^\textrm{\scriptsize1h}$ at room temperature. After the incubation, supernatant with magnetic separation was transferred into another tube containing $\mathsf{M B@U I O-66-N H_{2}}$ , followed by sequentially for $^{1\mathrm{h}}$ at room temperature. After centrifugation, supernatant was dropped on the premodified electrode surface. Subsequently, the improved electrode was statically processed for $30~\mathrm{{min}}$ to fully capture MB molecules. Finally, the square wave voltammetry measurement was conducted from $-1.0\mathrm{~V~}$ to $1.0\mathrm{~V~}$ in PBS buffer containing 0.1 M KCl $\mathrm{(pH=7.4}$ ).
## 3. Results and discussion
## 3.1. Principle of electrochemical immunosensor
The mechanism of immunoassay for detecting SCCA was illustrated in Scheme 1 and divided into the following two parts. (1) Immunoreaction. ZIF-8 encapsulated GOx was synthesized through one-step method, and lots of uniform Au NPs were in situ reduced on its surface to connect ${\mathsf{A b}}_{2}$ through the Au-S bonding between Au NP and the sulfydryl group of antibody [37– 38]. BSA then was employed to blocking remain activate sites. Analogously, $\mathrm{Fe}_{3}0_{4}$ were constructed using carbodiimide/succinimide chemistry. Ultimately, immunoreaction was expectedly implanted through an interaction of antigen–antibody. (2) Preparation of $\mathsf{M B@U I O-66-N H_{2}}$ . UIO-66- $\cdot\Nu\mathrm{H}_{2}$ was prepared through hydrothermal method and DNA1 was fixed on its surface using carbodiimide/succinimide chemistry similarly. MB as signal molecule was then sealed up for safekeeping inside the $\mathsf{U I O-}66\mathsf{-N H}_{2}$ and DNA2 was further coupled with DNA 1.
Protons can be produced under the catalysis of GOx when glucose was emerged in tube. After the transfer from proton to the solution containing dispersed $\mathsf{M B@U I O-66-N H_{2}}$ , stored MB molecules in UIO-66- $\left.\mathsf{N H}_{2}\right.$ was quickly released behind the collapse of hybrid duplex DNA1/DNA2, which was attributed that a cytosine (C)-rich sequence in DNA2 reconfigured at acidic pH into an imotif structure. For i-motif structure, they are four-stranded DNA secondary structures which can form in sequences rich in cytosine. With the presence of protons, they are comprised of two parallelstranded DNA duplexes held together in an antiparallel orientation by intercalated, cytosine–cytosine+ base pairs [39]. Later, the released MB molecules in the solution were affixed on the AurGO modified electrode surface through the $\pi{-}\pi$ interaction between Au-rGO and MB molecules, successfully constructing a signal-enriched electrochemical immunosensing interface to indicate the content of incubated antigen.
## 3.2. Characterization of materials
As shown in Fig. 1A, as-prepared ZIF-8 nanoparticle with a diameter of $\sim500\mathrm{nm}$ had a uniform dodecahedron structure. After the $\mathsf{N a A u C l}_{4}$ was reduced in situ on the ZIF-8 surface, many tiny Au NPs can be observed in Fig. 1B. Meanwhile, the morphologies of UIO-66- $\cdot\Nu\mathrm{H}_{2}$ and $\mathsf{M B@U I O-66-N H_{2}}$ were also observed in Fig. 1C and 1D, respectively. With a diameter of $\sim150\ \mathrm{nm}$ , the morphology of UIO- $66\substack{-\Delta\mathsf{N}}\mathsf{H}_{2}$ had no obvious changes after DNA functionalization and MB molecules loading. Subsequently, with the characterization of element mapping (excluding the elements such as O and $Z\mathrm{{r}}$ originally contained in UIO-66- $\cdot\Nu\mathrm{H}_{2}$ ), the presence of phosphorus peak was convectively proved that the successful modification of DNA (Fig. 1E-H). To further demonstrate the steps of DNA functionalization for UIO-66- $\cdot\Nu\mathrm{H}_{2}$ and in-situ reduction of Au NPs on ZIF-8 surface, XPS and EDS were used for the characterization of these nanomaterials. Compared with the pure UIO-66- $\mathsf{N H}_{2}$ (Fig. 2A and 2E), the phosphorus peak was all emerged in XPS and EDS spectra after the modification of DNA (Fig. 2B and 2F). Similarly, the presence of gold peaks in XPS and EDS spectra (Fig. 2C and 2G) can also prove the successful synthesis of $\mathtt{A u@Z I F-8}$ composites in comparison of pure ZIF-8 (Fig. 2D and 2H). Besides, the high-resolution of $\mathtt{Z n2p}$ and Zr 3d were further presented in Fig. 2I and 2 J respectively. The peaks of Zr $3\mathrm{d}_{5/2}$ , Zr $3\mathrm{d}_{3/2}$ were posited at 181.97 and $184.35\mathrm{eV}.$ , respectively. The peaks of Zn $2\mathsf{p}_{3/2}$ , Zn $2{\tt p}_{1/2}$ were located at 1022 and 1045 eV [40–41], respectively. The hydrodynamic sizes of $\mathsf{M B@U I O-66-N H_{2}}$ and ZIF-8 composites were also concluded about as 500 and $150~\mathrm{{nm}}$ in Fig. 2K and 2L.
Complementally, XRD spectra of $\mathsf{M B@U I O-66-N H_{2}}$ and $\operatorname{GOx@ZIF-8}$ were illustrated in Fig. 3C and Fig. 3D to characterize their composition. Compared with the simulation in Fig. 3C, modification of i-motif DNA strand and MB loading cannot affect the lattice of $\mathsf{U I O-}66\mathsf{-N H}_{2}$ . ZIF-8 encapsulated GOx also exhibited no differences than original ZIF-8 (Fig. 3D). Besides, successful preparation of $\mathsf{M B@U I O-66-N H_{2}}$ was indispensable to characterize with reliable methods including zeta potential and IR spectra, which can testify the modification at every step on the surface of UIO-66- ${\cdot\}\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ .
As shown in Fig. 3B, original UIO-66- $\cdot\Nu\mathrm{H}_{2}$ behaved positive charge ${\tt o f}+34.9{\mathrm{~mV}}$ due to the distribution of amino groups inside it. After the modification of DNA1 on its surface, negative charge of $-10.4~\mathrm{mV}$ had been naturally transferred due to the residues of DNA. As MB molecules loading and subsequent coupling of DNA2, negative charge had been changed into $-16.1\ \mathrm{mV}$ because of the negative property of MB. IR spectrum in Fig. 3E further proved its synthesis, new peaks including $883~\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ , $1037~\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ , $1135\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ justified the encapsulation of MB in UIO-66- ${\cdot}\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ . Meanwhile, IR spectrum of GOx@ZIF-8 had been also implanted in comparison of ZIF-8. New peaks at $1102~\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ and $1662~\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ were presented, which was ascribed to the successful encapsulation of GOx in ZIF-8 (Fig. 3F).
Electrode working surface area as an important parameter can affect the readout of electrochemical signal affirmatively so that it must be calculated to get a determined value. As depicted in Fig. 3G, the current response was proportional with the $\upsilon^{1/2}$ ( $\upsilon$ referred to the scan rate of CV) and a linear equation was calculated as $y=63.4728\mathrm{x}+2.4322$ $\mathrm{'R}^{2}=0.9949\rangle$ . According to the Randles-Sevcik equation [42–43]:
$$
I_{p}=2.69\times10^{5}n^{3/2}A D^{1/2}\upsilon^{1/2}C
$$
(where $I_{p}$ is the current value (A), $n$ is the number of electrons transferred, $\upsilon$ is the scan rate $(\mathsf{V}\mathsf{S}^{-1})$ , D is the diffusion coefficient, A is the electrode working surface area $(\mathsf{c m}^{2})$ and $c$ is the concentration of the electroactive substance $\mathrm{(mol~cm}^{-3}$ ). The electrode working surface area was calculated to be $0.0963~\mathrm{cm}^{-2}$ . The corresponding CV curves were shown in Fig. 3H.
3.3. Catalysis of ZIF-8 immunoprobe to glucose and responsive release of MB@UIO-66- ${N H}_{2}$
As shown in Fig. 3K and 3L, the catalysis of ZIF-8 immunoprobe to glucose was studied in detail through a monitor of pH change. Firstly, quantitative ZIF-8 immunoprobe solution was reacted with glucose and a monitor for time-dependent change of $\mathsf{p H}$ value was started from $0\ \mathrm{min}$ to $120\ \mathrm{\min}$ , resulting that pH value was decreased gradually as well as incremental protons were produced in this catalytic reaction (Fig. 3L). Besides, the catalysis effect of different ZIF-8 immunoprobe volume to glucose was also investigated as illustrated in Fig. 3K. Oxygenolysis rate of quantitative glucose can be gradually accelerated with the increased addition of immunoprobe volume after a time-specific incubation, which proved that the change of pH can be greatly proportional with the amount of immunoprobe. Controllable release process of MB molecules loaded in UIO-66- $\cdot\Nu\mathrm{H}_{2}$ was also justified with the square wave voltammetry (SWV) and UV–vis method. As depicted in Fig. 3I, the current responses were gradually increased with the incubation of $0,0.1\ 10\ \mathrm{ng\mL^{-1}}$ antigen, which was attributed to the release of more MB molecules from ${\mathsf{U I O-}}66{\mathsf{-N H}}_{2}$ . Meanwhile, UV–vis was employed as another auxiliary method to verify this mechanism and the result for the increased absorbance of released MB molecules can well be match with the foregoing SWV results (Fig. 3J).
## 3.4. Optimization of conditions
Optimization of experimental condition was an important portion in assembly of immunoassay to achieve optimal detection performance. Figure S1 gave the optimization of the incubation time for immune-recognition process. Current increased along with augment of incubation time and kept constant after $50~\mathrm{min}$ , indicating that the immune-recognition between antigen and antibody had achieved the saturation.
## 3.5. Detection performance of immunoassay
Proposed immunoassay owned excellent detection performance toward SCCA under the optimal condition. As shown in Fig. 4A, current response was gradually increased as the incubated antigen increased (The lines of a-h were corresponding to the incubation of 0, 0.001, 0.01, 0.1, 1, 10, 100 and $1000~\mathrm{{ng~mL^{-1}}}$ SCCA, respectively). Correspondingly, a good linear relationship was established between current response and SCCA concentration (Fig. 4B), obtaining regression equation of $\mathsf{I}=-10.0337\mathrm{lg}\mathsf{C}\mathrm{-}70.023$ 1 $\mathrm{~\boldmath~\tilde{~}{~R~}~}^{2}\mathrm{~\boldmath~\Gamma~}=\mathrm{~\boldmath~0.9994\rangle~}$ . Simultaneously, limitation of detection (LOD) was calculated to be 1.504 fg $\mathrm{m}\mathrm{L}^{-1}$ (the cut-off value of SCCA was $1.5~\mathrm{{ng}~\mathrm{{mL^{-1}}}}$ ), indicating that the immunoassay can accurately detect the content of SCCA. Compared with other immunoassay reported in previous literature (Table S1), it also had a wider detection range and lower LOD.
## 3.6. Specificity, stability and reproducibility of immunosensor
Miscellaneous interferences including NSE, CEA, PSA, AA, DA, and their mixture were adulterated in SCCA whose concentration was two orders of magnitude smaller than interferences to test the specificity of proposed immunosensor. As shown in Figure S2, doping of these interferences cannot cause significant negative effects for the detection of SCCA. Meanwhile, immune bioconjugates was also authenticated the stability in $4~^{\circ}\mathsf C$ refrigerator for a month (Figure S3). After testing every other week, current response still maintained $1.27\%$ on average. Reproducibility was also verified to be excellent by testing five different electrodes using the same treatment. As shown in Figure S4, related standard deviation (RSD) of responsive current generated by five electrodes was less than $1.6\%$ .
## 3.7. Detection in serum samples
Considered the detection performance of immunosensor in practical sample, human serum was employed as matrix to support different concentration of SCCA to be analyzed (diluted factor was calculated to be 10). As illustrated in Table 1, relative error was less than $6.67\%$ in comparison of a standard method (chemiluminescence immunoassay analyzer, CMIA), both of which were in acceptable range. All these results suggested that the proposed immunoassay had good practicability in clinical analysis.
## 4. Conclusion
In this work, a proton-responsive annunciator based on i-motif DNA structure modified UIO-66- $\left.\mathsf{N H}_{2}\right.$ was proposed for designing electrochemical immunosensing interface. With the surficial functionalization of nucleic acid, UIO-66- $\left.\mathrm{NH}_{2}\right.$ was successfully endowed with firm leakproofness for the physisorption of electroactive species, and this signal furnishing mode in solution can also efficaciously avoid the insulation of MOFs when it was directly applied in designing immunosensing interface on electrode. In comparison of most existed electrochemical immunosensing interface for SCCA detection, this amelioration has a better detection limit of 1.504 fg $\mathrm{mL}^{-1}$ . If this electrochemical immunosensing interface can be readout in a digital way and designed to be a portable device, it will have a prospect of wide application.
